LOCAL ISSUES MODE — two duties are YOURS here that GitHub normally does for free:
1. CLOSE SHIPPED ISSUES AT RELEASE. Nothing auto-closes a local issue when its commit reaches __DEFAULT__. After you release, list the shipped work (git log --oneline on the release range — squash subjects carry "(LI-<n>)") and close each: gh issue close <n> --comment "shipped in <version>". An open local issue whose work shipped poisons every survey downstream — close them the same tick you release.
2. MINE THE STORE FOR PROSE. The local tracker holds the factory's full design reasoning, and unlike GitHub it is invisible to PR readers — so when you (or anyone you brief) author a PR DESCRIPTION, a CHANGELOG entry, or DOCS, pull the reasoning out and cite it: `gh issue view <n> --comments` for one thread, `gh issue export <n> [<n>…]` for a markdown dump you can quote from. A PR against the upstream repo should carry the distilled WHY (the design decision and the alternatives rejected, from the issue thread) — not a bare diff description. Never reference the upstream repo's own GitHub issue numbers; your numbers are LI-<n>.
